Agreed. You'll get the goal only after task 34 freezes the list: the gate at `findings 0` against a complete crosswalk. Your quote also shows a second defect: "34, 33, then 3 through 28" was hand-written, and it leaves out the real positions of tasks 29–32. So the gate will compute the execution order and the goal will quote it. Adding that to the gate while the crosswalk agent finishes.

The gate's self-test passes, now including the ordering check. On the current task list it reports `findings 0`, and its computed order is: 33, 34, 3, 4, 29, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 30, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 27, 31, 32, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 28. That's only the consistency half. The coverage half needs the crosswalk, which the agent is still building. When it reports, I'll:

1. run the gate with the crosswalk;
2. fix every finding in its owning task, and swap the remaining `LoomEnhancements.md` citations for code references;
3. re-run the gate until it prints `findings 0`;
4. mark task 34 complete, which freezes the list;
5. then give you the goal, quoting the order the gate prints.

Understood: when the worker finishes I'll file its report and update task 14's status, then pause the ledger and leave the job alone until you ask. Until then nothing new starts. I won't run the ledger's 13 checks or commit after its report, and no further worker is dispatched.

You also asked for the goal in the `/goal` hook. Here it is as the hook states it:

> Milestone a of Loom 1.0.0 (tasks 10, 14, 21, 22) is delivered under the job ledger as `Tools/Context-Engineering/loomrefinements.md` specifies, which is read-only and the only source of requirements. It is met when the most recent output shows all of:
> 1. `ledger.mjs next` printing `MILESTONE a COMPLETE`;
> 2. `ledger.mjs verify` printing `findings 0`;
> 3. the plan gate printing `findings 0`;
> 4. `git status --porcelain` in Tools/loom printing nothing, and `rev-list --left-right --count origin/main...HEAD` printing `0 0`;
> 5. the superproject's `git log -1` showing the Tools/loom pointer advanced to the last milestone-a commit and pushed;
> 6. the spec's SHA-256 printing `8139f60d0b132405ebe02124e5553cb3085a5113edb6b1d87ef096e4f199f50e`.
>
> It also ends, with the turn stating what it waits for, when `ledger next` first prints `LEDGER PAUSED`, a task sits in `decision` awaiting the owner, or a command reports `HOST_BUSY` after its wait limit. Every turn ends by printing `ledger next`.

The pause you've asked for ends the loop through that `LEDGER PAUSED` clause, not because milestone a is complete. Only task 10 is committed. Task 14's work will be finished but uncommitted, and tasks 21 and 22 haven't started.

The final-round worker is still running. The ledger shows task 14 `[briefed]` on dispatch `4976b906…`, and I'm waiting for that worker's report, then the pause.
